NNAAMMEE
     nnssccdd — name service caching daemon

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     nnssccdd [--ddnnsstt] [--ii _c_a_c_h_e_n_a_m_e] [--II _c_a_c_h_e_n_a_m_e]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The nnssccdd utility is the system caching daemon.  It can cache almost all
     types of data and is basically intended to be used with the nnsssswwiittcchh sub‐
     system.  The cache is actually per-user.  This means that each user can
     work only with the cached data that were cached by themselves, and cannot
     poison the cache of other users.  The nnssccdd utility supports two types of
     caching:

     TTyyppee  DDeessccrriippttiioonn

     Common caching
           Each cached element is the key+value pair.  This type of caching
           supports policies which are applied when maximum number of cached
           elements is exceeded.  Three policies are available: FFIIFFOO (first in
           - first out), LLRRUU (least recently used) and LLFFUU (least frequently
           used).  This type of caching is used with the ggeettXXXXXXbbyynnaammee() family
           of functions.

     Multipart caching
           Each cached element is the part of the elements sequence.  This
           type of caching is intended to be used with the ggeettXXXXXXeenntt() family
           of functions.

     The nnssccdd utility is able not only to cache elements, but to perform the
     actual nsswitch lookups by itself.  To enable this feature, use the
     _p_e_r_f_o_r_m_-_a_c_t_u_a_l_-_l_o_o_k_u_p_s parameter in nscd.conf(5).

     The nnssccdd utility recognizes the following runtime options:

     --nn      Do not daemonize; nnssccdd will not fork or disconnect itself from
             the terminal.

     --ss      Single-threaded mode.  Forces using only one thread for all pro‐
             cessing purposes (it overrides the _t_h_r_e_a_d_s parameter in the
             nscd.conf(5) file).

     --tt      Trace mode.  All trace messages will be written to stdout.  This
             mode is usually used with --nn and --ss flags are used for debugging
             purposes.

     --ii _c_a_c_h_e_n_a_m_e
             Invalidates personal cache.  When specified, nnssccdd acts as the ad‐
             ministration tool.  It asks the already running nnssccdd to invali‐
             date the specified part of the cache of the calling user.  For
             example, sometimes you may want to invalidate your “hosts” cache.
             You can specify “all” as the _c_a_c_h_e_n_a_m_e to invalidate your per‐
             sonal cache as a whole.  You cannot use this option for the
             _c_a_c_h_e_n_a_m_e for which the _p_e_r_f_o_r_m_-_a_c_t_u_a_l_-_l_o_o_k_u_p_s option is enabled.

     --II _c_a_c_h_e_n_a_m_e
             Invalidates the cache for every user.  When specified, nnssccdd acts
             as the administration tool.  It asks the already running nnssccdd to
             invalidate the specified part of the cache for every user.  You
             can specify “all” as the _c_a_c_h_e_n_a_m_e to invalidate the whole cache.
             Only the root can use this option.

